{
  "id": "path-sum-ii",
  "questionId": "path-sum-ii",
  "slug": "path-sum-ii",
  "title": "Path Sum II",
  "difficulty": "Medium",
  "category": "Tree dfs",
  "patterns": [
    "tree",
    "dfs",
    "backtracking"
  ],
  "lists": [],
  "hints": [
    "this is path-sum but instead of just answering yes/no, you need to reconstruct and save each qualifying path - what extra state do you need to carry through the recursion?",
    "carry a mutable list representing the current path from the root down to here, appending the current node's value before recursing into children",
    "when you reach a leaf, check whether the running sum equals targetSum - if so, save a COPY of the current path (not a reference to the mutable list, since it keeps changing)",
    "after exploring both children of a node, remove that node's value from the path before returning to the parent - this 'backtracking' step is what lets the same list be reused across different branches"
  ],
  "commonMistakes": [
    "appending the shared mutable path list directly to the results instead of a copy, so all saved paths end up reflecting the final state of the list instead of the state at the time they were found",
    "forgetting to backtrack (remove the current value) after recursing into both children, which corrupts the path for sibling branches",
    "checking the leaf condition before or without checking both children are actually missing, again risking a false leaf on a node with only one child"
  ],
  "content": {
    "problemStatement": "Binary trees are encoded as flat arrays using complete-binary-tree (heap) indexing:\n\n- the root sits at index 0\n- a node at index `i` has its left child at index `2*i+1` and right child at index `2*i+2`\n- a child index only counts as real if it's within the array's bounds **and** its value isn't the sentinel `-101`\n- `-101` means \"no node there\" - real node values always fall between -100 and 100, so -101 never collides with a genuine value\n\nFor example, `[1, 2, 3, -101, 4]` encodes this tree:\n\n```\n      1\n     / \\\n    2   3\n     \\\n      4\n```\n\nNode 2's left child is missing (sentinel) but its right child is 4.\n\nYou're also given a target sum, `targetSum`.\n\n**Find every root-to-leaf path (a leaf is a node with no children) whose values add up exactly to `targetSum`, and return all such paths, each as a list of values in root-to-leaf order.**\n\nIf no path qualifies, return an empty list.\n",
    "constraints": [
      "0 <= number of real nodes <= 5000",
      "every real node value is between -100 and 100 inclusive",
      "targetSum fits in a normal int range",
      "the sentinel value -101 marks a missing node and never appears as a real value",
      "each returned path lists values in root-to-leaf order; if no path matches, return an empty outer list"
    ],
    "notes": [
      "empty tree ([-101]), which always returns an empty list",
      "multiple distinct paths sharing a common prefix that both sum to targetSum",
      "a node with only one child, whose missing side must not be treated as a valid leaf"
    ],
    "inputFormat": "Arguments passed into solution function",
    "outputFormat": "Return output matching method signature",
    "sampleTestCases": [
      {
        "id": "sample_1",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "tree = [5, 4, 8, 11, -101, 13, 4, 7, 2, -101, -101, -101, -101, 5, 1], targetSum = 22",
        "output": "[[5, 4, 11, 2], [5, 8, 4, 5]]",
        "expectedOutput": "[[5, 4, 11, 2], [5, 8, 4, 5]]",
        "explanation": "root 5 branches into 4 (with children 11, missing; 11 has children 7, 2) and 8 (with children 13, 4; that 4 has children 5, 1). two root-to-leaf paths sum to 22: 5+4+11+2=22 and 5+8+4+5=22"
      },
      {
        "id": "sample_2",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "tree = [5, 4, 8, 11, -101, 13, 4, 7, 2, -101, -101, -101, -101, 5, 1], targetSum = 26",
        "output": "[[5, 8, 13]]",
        "expectedOutput": "[[5, 8, 13]]",
        "explanation": "5+8+13=26, and 13 is a leaf since it has no children in this tree"
      },
      {
        "id": "sample_3",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "tree = [1], targetSum = 1",
        "output": "[[1]]",
        "expectedOutput": "[[1]]",
        "explanation": "a single node is both the root and a leaf at once, so the path is just [1]"
      }
    ],
